= Why $R L R′ = L$

R turns the right face and L turns the left face, so the two turns are of opposite faces. Opposite faces commute: the sequence R then L reaches the same state as L then R. Write the two sequences out:

#align(center)[
  R, then L, then R′
  #h(1.2em)
  L, then R, then R′
]

The first equals the second because R and L may be swapped. In the second, R followed by R′ cancels, and L is what remains.

The site's code verified the identity. On the supplied maps of the 54 slots the same fact is direct, with no extra count attached. Four applications of R return every sticker to its own slot, so the inverse of R is R applied three times. Applying R, then L, then that inverse agrees with L on every slot. Applying R then L agrees with L then R on every slot.
